ropose a powerful, fully automated, and numerically robust algorithm to compute (inverse) equilibrium bid functions for asymmetric, Independent Private Values, First-Price auctions. The algorithm relies upon a built-in algebra of local Taylor-series expansions in order to compute highly accurate solutions to the set of differential equations characterizing first order conditions.\"\n\nThis is a relevant source. But we also need to find the general method. The user likely wants a description: \"In a first-price sealed-bid auction with two bidders with independent private values drawn from different distributions, the equilibrium bidding functions are given by solving a system of integral/differential equations; the solution is given by a system of ODEs: b_i(v) = v - (1 - F_i(v)) / f_i(v) * E[...]. Actually, the known result: In a first price auction with n=2, the symmetric equilibrium is b(v) = v - (1 - F(v))/f(v). For asymmetric case, the equilibrium is not symmetric, but can be found as the unique solution to a system of first-order condition ODEs: b_1(v1) = v1 - (1 - F_1(v1)) / f_1(v1) * (F_2(b_2^{-1}(b_1(v1))) / (d/dv1 b_2^{-1}(b_1(v1)))? Something like that.\n\nLet's search for \"asymmetric first price auction two bidders equilibrium bid function\" and \"Lebrun 1999\" which is a key paper.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": [\"asymmetric first price auction two bidders equilibrium bid function\", \"Lebrun 1999 first price auction asymmetric\", \"first price auction asymmetric equilibrium two bidders\"]}}\n</tool_call>", "total_time_ms": 5349.820852279663, "rtt_ms": 18.826802571614582, "inference_time_ms": 5330.994049708049}
